data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is needed here.**

Your code only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — none of which are affected by the Thread.Abort removal in .NET 6+. These APIs work identically in modern .NET. The code will compile and run correctly as-is on .NET 8.

---

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration):

If you'd like to modernize the parallelism, you could replace manual threads with `Parallel.ForEach`:

```csharp
public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items)
{
    Parallel.ForEach(items, item =>
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    });
}
```

Or with `Task.WhenAll` for async-friendly code. But again, these are **optional improvements** — your existing code requires zero changes for the .NET 8 migration.
